# QSORT(3)

## NAME

qsort - quicker sort

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

void qsort(void *base, size_t nel, size_t width, int (*compar)(const void *, const void *));
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Qsort* is an implementation of the quicker-sort algorithm. The first argument is a pointer to
the base of the data; the second is the number of elements; the third is the width of an
element in bytes; the last is the name of the comparison routine to be called with two
arguments which are pointers to the elements being compared. The routine must return an integer
less than, equal to, or greater than 0 according as the first argument is to be considered less
than, equal to, or greater than the second.

## SEE ALSO

sort(1), string(3)

## BESM-6 NOTES

### The width is in bytes, and a word is six of them

So sorting an array of **int** means **width = sizeof(int) = 6**, not 2 and not 4. A page that
says otherwise is quoting a different machine.

### The pointer comparisons are safe, and that is not luck

*Qsort* compares partition cursors (**i < lp**, **j > hp**) and takes a difference (**l - a**),
and a fat **char \*** does *not* sort as a plain word: incrementing one **decreases** its 3-bit
byte offset, which sits above the word address.

**The compiler knows.** It lowers a relational between two fat pointers through **b$pdiff**,
the same helper as **-**, and tests the sign. So this file is v7's arithmetic, unchanged. See
getpass(3) for the case where the same operator *does* give the wrong answer, and why.

### The exchanges go a word at a time when they can

This is the one thing this file adds to v7, and the alignment test is expressible in C: casting
a **char \*** to an **int \*** discards the byte offset, and casting back rebuilds it as byte
\#0, so **the round trip is the identity exactly for a pointer that was already at byte #0.**
Two such pointers and a width that is a whole number of words mean the swap can move words.

### The comparison lives in a file-scope static, as v7 has it

That is worth recording because for a time it could not. A toolchain defect made a call through
a file-scope function pointer fail, and the routine had to carry its comparison through a
parameter instead. It is fixed upstream, and the structural change it forced is gone.

The durable fact underneath: a call through a function pointer is a **wtc** of the pointer and
a bare **13 vjm**, wherever the pointer lives - a parameter, an auto, an array element or a
file-scope variable. **wtc** carries a 15-bit address, so it reaches a global with no address
escape, and a callback needs no special handling at all.

